Multiple pro-PTI accounts and digital media outlets have been sharing a video across social media platforms since Sunday, claiming that a &ldquo;Free Imran Khan&rdquo; billboard had recently appeared in New York amid calls for the incarcerated PTI founder&rsquo;s release. However, the billboard shown in the viral video is not recent and has been circulating online since October 2025.
Imran has remained incarcerated since August 2023 following his arrest in connection with multiple cases. His continued imprisonment and calls for his release have remained a major political issue in Pakistan, with PTI supporters and the Pakistani diaspora periodically organising demonstrations and displaying &ldquo;Free Imran Khan&rdquo; messages in different countries.
Against this backdrop, images and videos purporting to show recent international support for him can attract significant attention, particularly when they are presented as evidence of a new development in his release campaign.
How it started
On Sunday, an account that appears to be pro-PTI based on its profile picture and past posts shared a video on X showing a roadside billboard displaying Imran&rsquo;s picture alongside the words, &ldquo;Free Imran Khan,&rdquo; with the caption: &ldquo;New York USA!&rdquo;
The post gained 30,900 views.
Another pro-PTI account on Monday also shared the same video on X with the following caption: &ldquo;Just now, someone from America sent a video of Imran Khan&rsquo;s big banner, Free Imran Khan Right now, there&rsquo;s only one news across the whole world, Imran Khan&rsquo;s release!!&rdquo;
The post gained 32,000 views.
A Pakistani digital media account on Instagram also shared the same video on the same day, with the caption: &ldquo;A billboard in New York, USA, has displayed a &lsquo;Free Imran Khan&rsquo; message, drawing attention on social media and among supporters of the former Pakistani prime minister.&rdquo;
The post gained 482,000 views.
The official PTI Gilgit-Baltistan account on X also shared the same video on September 1 with the caption: &ldquo;A &lsquo;Free Imran Khan&rsquo; billboard has been displayed in the New York and New Jersey area, organised by the Pakistani American diaspora to demand justice and call for his release,&rdquo; gaining more than 200 views.
The video was subsequently shared by several other pro-PTI accounts on X, as can be seen here, here, here, here, here, here, here, here, here, and here, collectively gaining more than 10,000 views.
The video was also shared by local digital media outlets on social media platforms, Instagram and Facebook, as can be seen here, here, here, and here.
Methodology
A fact-check was initiated to determine the veracity of the claim due to its high virality and keen public interest surrounding reports and social media posts about calls for Imran&rsquo;s release.
A keyword search was conducted to verify whether any credible Pakistani media outlets or official central PTI platforms had recently reported that a billboard displaying Imran&rsquo;s picture alongside a &ldquo;Free Imran Khan&rdquo; message had been placed in the US. However, no recent reports or official party statements confirming the claim or the visuals were found.
The banner in the viral clip featured the following organisation&rsquo;s name and website: First Pakistan Global.
A reverse image search subsequently conducted to trace the source of the viral video yielded a similar clip in an X post shared by the organisation on October 2, 2025, featuring the same billboard.
The post carried the caption: &ldquo;Voice of a nation free Imran Khan. This is not just a billboard. It&rsquo;s a megaphone for 250 million silenced voices.&rdquo;
The caption further stated that the billboard was located on Pulaski Highway in New Jersey and was being seen by millions of drivers.
&nbsp;
&nbsp;
&nbsp;
&nbsp;
View this post on Instagram
&nbsp;
&nbsp;
&nbsp;
&nbsp;
&nbsp;
&nbsp;
&nbsp;
&nbsp;
&nbsp;
&nbsp;
&nbsp;
The organisation described itself as a non-profit organisation comprised of entities and members residing abroad, &ldquo;dedicated to advocating for the betterment of Pakistan&rdquo;.
Furthermore, there was no recent post or share by the group that said or indicated that it had again put up the billboard.
Another post shared on X on the same date also featured the same billboard and carried the caption: &ldquo;Free Imran Khan billboards popping up again in the USA,&rdquo; further indicating that the footage was not recent but older, from October 2025.
Free Imran Khan billboards popping up again in the USA! pic.twitter.com/fI1qCFOwMf
&mdash; Adil Raja (@soldierspeaks) October 2, 2025
Further searches yielded an Instagram post by a local digital media outlet, News PMC, dated January 21, 2026, featuring the same clip as the viral video.
This confirmed that the video was not recent and was at least as old as January 2026.
🚨 VOICE OF A NATION 🚨
FREE IMRAN KHAN
This is not just a billboard.
It&rsquo;s a megaphone for 250 million silenced voices.
Pakistan&rsquo;s most popular leader has been jailed for over 785 days &mdash; and still stands with his people.
Dictators &amp; corrupt elites have put the country up for&hellip; pic.twitter.com/IjpK1oh6uI
&mdash; FirstPakistanGlobal (@FirstPakGlobal) October 1, 2025
Fact-check status: misleading
The claim that a viral video shows a recently installed &ldquo;Free Imran Khan&rdquo; billboard in New York, USA, is misleading.
The video is not recent and the billboard is from October 2025, when it was displayed on the Pulaski Highway in New Jersey.
This fact check was originally published by iVerify Pakistan &mdash; a project of CEJ-IBA and UNDP.

Actor Amna Ilyas has criticised self-proclaimed online &ldquo;critics&rdquo; for using their platforms to police women&rsquo;s choices, warning that such commentary can reinforce misogynistic attitudes and even validate violence against women offline.
Taking to Instagram, Ilyas said a recent dance performance of hers had predictably drawn both praise and criticism, something she said she had no issue with. What prompted her response, however, was coming across a &ldquo;self-acclaimed critic&rdquo; who called her &ldquo;cheap&rdquo;.
Rather than dismissing the comment outright, Ilyas said she looked through his content to understand the basis of his criticism and found what she described as a recurring pattern. &ldquo;All his videos talked about women that he didn't like or had some problems with; he is moral-policing them,&rdquo; she said.
She argued that such content promotes &ldquo;the ideology that a woman should live according to a man's vision&rdquo;, turning criticism of individual women into something more damaging. &ldquo;You have a mic, you have a platform, and you chose to instigate violence against women using this platform,&rdquo; she said.
&nbsp;
&nbsp;
&nbsp;
&nbsp;
View this post on Instagram
&nbsp;
&nbsp;
&nbsp;
&nbsp;
&nbsp;
&nbsp;
&nbsp;
&nbsp;
&nbsp;
&nbsp;
&nbsp;
Ilyas said she personally has the privilege of being able to disregard such criticism. &ldquo;It doesn't affect me because at the end of the day I live life the way I want, I don't care,&rdquo; she said.
But, she argued, the consequences can be far more serious for women who do not have the same freedom or support.
&ldquo;A woman who is already facing violence and is living life through extreme difficulty, your words will in turn validate the mindset of a man that's doing that with the woman, that whatever he's doing with that woman is right, because you have made it seem okay,&rdquo; she said.
For Ilyas, the problem therefore extends beyond an actor being insulted online. She said the reach of such commentary can reinforce attitudes that women already facing abuse and social restrictions are struggling against.
She ended by taking aim at those she believes profit from provoking outrage online. &ldquo;Congratulations, that for some views, some money, you have given men a licence to not let a woman lead a life with her own wishes,&rdquo; she said.
&nbsp;
Zhalay Sarhadi challenges the &lsquo;critic&rsquo; label
Actor Zhalay Sarhadi later backed Ilyas&rsquo;s argument in her own video, rejecting the idea that people who repeatedly target women online should be described simply as critics.
&ldquo;The people out there that are sitting there calling themselves &lsquo;critics,&rsquo; they are not critics, they are misogynists especially against women,&rdquo; she said.
Sarhadi questioned why gaining virality should come at the expense of taking what she described as &ldquo;such a detrimental stance against women&rdquo;.
She also pointed to the constant online scrutiny of women&rsquo;s &ldquo;clothing&rdquo; and &ldquo;choices&rdquo;, describing the commentary as &ldquo;so damaging&rdquo;. Like Ilyas, she said the effects are not limited to the women being directly targeted.
&ldquo;The non-privileged women that are already going through so much because of the society and their home situation, trying to overcome these obstacles&rdquo; can end up discouraged from &ldquo;wanting to move forward&rdquo; when they see even privileged women being targeted in this way, she said.
