Pakistan captain Babar Azam said his team must take greater responsibility and avoid another batting collapse when they face England in the second Test at Lord&rsquo;s on Thursday.
Pakistan were beaten by an innings and 103 runs in the opening Test at Headingley, but Babar said the team had reviewed its shortcomings and was focused on producing a stronger performance.
&ldquo;We did not play up to the mark in the last match,&rdquo; Babar told reporters on Wednesday. &ldquo;But we sat down and discussed. We discussed our weak points.&rdquo;
Babar, who missed the first Test, said Pakistan would approach the match session by session and insisted the team could respond strongly after a heavy defeat.
&ldquo;Whenever something like this happens, as a team, we always step up,&rdquo; he said. &ldquo;We play different cricket. We don&rsquo;t play like we did last time. But this time, you will see something different.&rdquo;
The Pakistan captain said the grass covering the Lord&rsquo;s pitch could influence the final team selection, with the tourists considering whether to field three fast bowlers and one specialist spinner.
&ldquo;We will see the final look of the pitch today,&rdquo; Babar said. &ldquo;When we go, we will decide which 11 will be suitable here.&rdquo;
He also stressed the importance of partnerships, particularly in England, where conditions can make life difficult for new batters.
&ldquo;If a set batsman is playing, it is a little easy for him,&rdquo; he said. &ldquo;But when a new batsman comes, it is a little difficult for him to build a partnership.&rdquo;
Pakistan have also endured a series of collapses, and Babar acknowledged that the team needed to handle the transition between set and new batters better.
&ldquo;Mostly, the set batsman gets out. We will lag there. It is difficult for a new batsman,&rdquo; he said. &ldquo;We will try to focus more on that situation.&rdquo;
Babar defended the decision to keep Abdullah Shafique in his current batting position rather than move him back to the opening slot.
&ldquo;He should play on his number,&rdquo; Babar said, adding that Shafique had also batted in that position in domestic cricket and was confident there.
The captain said senior players, including Mohammad Rizwan, had a responsibility to deliver, but rejected the idea that the wicketkeeper-batter was under additional pressure.
&ldquo;It is not a burden. It is a responsibility as a senior player,&rdquo; Babar said. &ldquo;It is not just Rizwan&rsquo;s responsibility. It is everyone&rsquo;s responsibility.&rdquo;
Babar also confirmed he was close to full fitness after missing the first Test, saying he had completed practice sessions and was &ldquo;90%&rdquo; fit for Thursday&rsquo;s match.
The three-Test series offers Pakistan a chance to recover from their disappointing start, while England can take an unassailable 2-0 lead with victory at Lord&rsquo;s.
Babar said playing at the historic ground remained a special occasion and urged his players to embrace the challenge.
&ldquo;It&rsquo;s an honour to play here at Lord&rsquo;s,&rdquo; he said. &ldquo;We are looking forward.&rdquo;
